Close the link(2) -EXDEV existence oracle.

  link(p, "/data/local/tmp/x")

returns -EXDEV for a hidden path (the source is on a different mount from the
target, always true for /system vs /data on Android) where an absent name gives
-ENOENT. The weakest of the four oracles by information content -- it yields one
bit and no path or label -- but it is a complete substitute for the other three,
so leaving it open would undo them. Measured on OP15 (Android 16, 6.12.23)
against all 24 blocked uids with a 7020-path genuinely-absent control and zero
false positives.

WHY THE ENGINE CANNOT ANSWER IT

do_linkat() resolves the source with filename_lookup() (older trees:
user_path_at()) and performs NO permission check on the final component, then
tests old_path.mnt != new_path.mnt and returns -EXDEV -- all before may_linkat()
and security_path_link() run. Nothing the engine hijacks is consulted. Verified
against v6.12 fs/namei.c.

WHERE THE GUARD GOES, AND WHY THERE

Immediately after the source lookup succeeds, before filename_create() builds
the target dentry -- so the guard also runs before the target directory is
touched at all, which is what an absent source would have done. Failing later,
at the -EXDEV test, would already have created and held the new dentry.

The error path is the function's own: on 5.15+ `goto out_putpath`, which
path_put()s old_path exactly once; on older trees a direct path_put() + return,
matching the shape those trees use. For the placement argument -- why an
EXISTENCE cloak belongs ahead of the file-level permission check, the opposite
of common/_hook/'s rule -- see the header of ghost_o_path.patch.

RISK NOTE

This is fs/namei.c, but it is not the lookup path: do_linkat() is reached only
by link(2)/linkat(2), which no Android hot path uses. The guard adds nothing to
any other syscall.

NOT applicable to 6.13+: do_linkat() no longer exists on torvalds/master (the
linkat path was reworked alongside filename_unlinkat()/filename_symlinkat()).
No patch is written for it because the builder fleet pins nothing above 6.12.

APPLIES TO

Fallback for trees where do_linkat() still resolves the source with
user_path_at() and returns directly. Verified at fuzz 0 against vanilla v4.9,
v4.14, v4.19, v5.4 and v5.10, and against the pinned OnePlus android12-5.10
tree. On v4.9/v4.14 the body is inside SYSCALL_DEFINE5(linkat, ...) rather
than a separate do_linkat(); the hunk is identical because it is anchored on
the lookup, not the signature.

--- a/fs/namei.c
+++ b/fs/namei.c
@@ -4242,6 +4242,7 @@
 	struct inode *delegated_inode = NULL;
 	int how = 0;
 	int error;
+	extern bool ghost_hidden_path(const struct path *gp);
 
 	if ((flags & ~(AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW | AT_EMPTY_PATH)) != 0)
 		return -EINVAL;
@@ -4262,6 +4263,15 @@
 	error = user_path_at(olddfd, oldname, how, &old_path);
 	if (error)
 		return error;
+	/*
+	 * do_linkat() returns -EXDEV on the mount mismatch before
+	 * may_linkat() runs, so the engine never sees this path. -EXDEV
+	 * proves the name exists; an absent name gives -ENOENT here.
+	 */
+	if (ghost_hidden_path(&old_path)) {
+		path_put(&old_path);
+		return -ENOENT;
+	}
 
 	new_dentry = user_path_create(newdfd, newname, &new_path,
 					(how & LOOKUP_REVAL));
